@meta
id: 20-13
title: The system clipboard
chapter: 20
type: lesson
skills: register-clipboard
par: 34
minutes: 6
vscode: Ctrl-C, Ctrl-V — this is the one register VS Code has
open: .env.example README.md

@brief
Two registers reach outside vim. `"+` is the system clipboard, the one
Ctrl-C and Ctrl-V use in every other program. `"*` is, on Linux, the
primary selection — whatever you last highlighted with the mouse, pasted
by the middle button. On macOS and Windows the two are the same thing.
`"+yy` copies a line for the browser; `"+p` pastes what the browser
copied.

The README's configuration section should show the exact
`PAYMENT_GATEWAY_URL` line from `.env.example`, inside the empty code
block. Copy it across through the clipboard register, by name, so you
have done it once on purpose.

@keys
| `"+yy` | yank the line to the system clipboard |
| `"+p` | put from the system clipboard |
| `"*` | the primary selection, where that is a separate thing |
| `:reg +*` | see what both hold |
| `:set clipboard?` | how this vim is wired up |

@hints
`/GATEWAY` Enter, `"+yy`. Then `:bn` to the README and find the opening
fence.
--
`/sh` Enter lands on the ```` ```sh ```` line. `"+p` puts the line
below it.
--
`/GATEWAY` Enter `"+yy` `:bn` Enter `/sh` Enter `"+p` `:wqa` Enter.

@solution
    /GATEWAY<Enter> "+yy  :bn<Enter> /sh<Enter> "+p  :wqa<Enter>

Now the part that matters on this machine. Your vimrc has
`clipboard=unnamedplus`, which makes the unnamed register and `"+` the
same thing: every `yy` is a `"+yy`, every `p` is a `"+p`. That is why
copying between vim and a browser has worked all along without you
naming a register, and it is why this lesson would have passed with a
plain `yy` and `p`.

It has a cost, and you met it in lesson 20-01. If the clipboard is the
unnamed register, then anything copied anywhere on the machine lands in
`""` — a URL from the browser, a snippet from a chat window — and the
next `p` in vim puts that instead of the line you yanked. `"0` ignores
the clipboard and holds your last yank in vim, which is the real reason
to have it in your fingers. And `"_` still deletes into nothing, even
when nothing means "not the clipboard either".

On a machine without the setting, `y` and `p` stay inside vim and `"+`
is the only way out. `:set clipboard?` tells you which world you are in.
Ssh into a server and the answer is usually the second one, plus no
clipboard at all — vim will silently use the unnamed register in place
of `"+`, and `"+yy` there is just `yy`.

@start .env.example
DATABASE_URL=mysql://root:root@database:3306/orders
PAYMENT_GATEWAY_URL=https://gateway.payments.example/v2
PAYMENT_GATEWAY_TIMEOUT_MS=5000
EMAIL_FROM=orders@example.com

